`src/game.ts` exports `game`, a `GameDefinition<CascadeDebugApi>`: the game
instance class, a level registry holding the single level the game runs in, and
`startLevel` naming it. The game opens that one level and never opens another;
every screen is a value of the state's `screen` field, so the world and its game
state live for the whole session. The instance's `initialize` defines the cues
and returns the debug surface. The level's game mode runs the screens and the
rules, and it adds a single player in its `beginPlay`, possessing nothing; that
player's controller is where the pointer is read. The mode's `gameStateClass` is
`CascadeState`, so the world's game state is the state `specs/state.md` declares.
The actors, components, and controllers you write draw that state and drive it.

